WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.429
The task keeps all of Edlib except

2
00:00:02.491 --> 00:00:04.057
the body of one function

3
00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:06.060
the one that recovers the path.

4
00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.044
The agent gets the query

5
00:00:08.044 --> 00:00:10.921
the target interval, their reversed copies

6
00:00:10.921 --> 00:00:12.490
and the exact distance.

7
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.097
It must return an optimal path as

8
00:00:15.165 --> 00:00:17.080
an array of operation bytes.

9
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.889
Nothing outside the function may change

10
00:00:19.889 --> 00:00:22.287
and it may not touch files, the clock

11
00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:24.070
other processes, or threads.

12
00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.299
There are sixty workloads: two lengths

13
00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:29.744
five similarities, the three modes

14
00:00:29.744 --> 00:00:31.850
and public and held-out seeds.

15
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.243
Every output is checked: the exact distance

16
00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:36.696
the exact locations

17
00:00:36.696 --> 00:00:38.840
and a valid path of that cost.

18
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.150
A wrong answer anywhere scores zero.

19
00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.774
Each workload runs fifteen times with the path

20
00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:48.355
and fifteen times with locations only

21
00:00:48.355 --> 00:00:50.010
each in a fresh process.

22
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.193
The reconstruction time is the fastest path

23
00:00:53.259 --> 00:00:55.960
run minus the fastest locations-only run.

24
00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.950
That difference is small and noisy.

25
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.346
In our own model

26
00:01:00.407 --> 00:01:02.437
a true time of five hundredths of

27
00:01:02.498 --> 00:01:04.712
a millisecond comes out not positive

28
00:01:04.774 --> 00:01:06.373
on six workloads of sixty

29
00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:08.710
and a difference that is not positive

30
00:01:08.771 --> 00:01:09.940
fails the workload.

31
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.025
In this run

32
00:01:11.086 --> 00:01:13.413
the human reference averaged only zero

33
00:01:13.474 --> 00:01:15.250
point eight six milliseconds.

34
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.394
Memory is the peak of the

35
00:01:17.457 --> 00:01:19.897
reconstruction call, counting the heap

36
00:01:19.897 --> 00:01:22.150
the stack and writable static data.

37
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.015
To pass

38
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.527
a run must be strictly faster than the

39
00:01:25.592 --> 00:01:27.717
reference and use no more memory

40
00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:30.100
on every one of the sixty workloads.

41
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.210
The score is separate.

42
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.167
It rewards a short time for the whole run

43
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.588
most of which the agent cannot change

44
00:01:37.588 --> 00:01:39.760
so good runs score close together.

45
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:43.070
The reference scores sixty-five point five.

46
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.170
Pause and predict.

47
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.306
A run's reconstruction takes three tenths of

48
00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:50.901
a millisecond less than the reference's

49
00:01:50.965 --> 00:01:52.674
but on one workload it uses

50
00:01:52.737 --> 00:01:54.320
one kibibyte more memory.

51
00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:55.790
Does it pass?

52
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.140
No: memory has no tolerance.

53
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.489
Yet its score goes up a little

54
00:02:04.489 --> 00:02:06.810
because the score counts only time.

55
00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.050
One model of sixteen passed.

56
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.019
Gemini 4 Argon, running in Antigravity

57
00:02:13.086 --> 00:02:14.764
won on time and on memory

58
00:02:14.831 --> 00:02:16.376
on all sixty workloads

59
00:02:16.443 --> 00:02:18.121
with under a third of the

60
00:02:18.189 --> 00:02:19.464
reference's memory

61
00:02:19.464 --> 00:02:21.680
and scored sixty-six point nine.

62
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.051
GPT-6 Astra had the fastest

63
00:02:24.126 --> 00:02:25.547
positive mean time

64
00:02:25.547 --> 00:02:28.092
but lost on time on one workload

65
00:02:28.092 --> 00:02:28.990
and failed.

66
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.540
GPT-6.1 Terra's mean time is below zero

67
00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.060
on many workloads its path runs were faster

68
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.460
than its locations-only runs.

69
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.570
That is the noise at work.

70
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.824
Claude Opus 5.5 wrote the solution

71
00:02:43.903 --> 00:02:45.790
the task page describes.

72
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.323
One banded pass saves checkpoints

73
00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:50.056
and strips are traced back

74
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.340
from right to left.

75
00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.031
For similar pairs it takes a shortcut

76
00:02:54.031 --> 00:02:55.324
diagonal transition

77
00:02:55.386 --> 00:02:57.541
which slides along runs of matching

78
00:02:57.603 --> 00:02:58.650
letters for free.

79
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.290
On our own ninety-nine percent pair

80
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.771
it takes about fourteen hundred steps

81
00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:05.742
where the band took sixty-four

82
00:03:05.806 --> 00:03:06.760
thousand cells.

83
00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.349
But its peak memory

84
00:03:08.411 --> 00:03:09.897
one hundred seventy-four

85
00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:11.260
kibibytes on average

86
00:03:11.322 --> 00:03:13.304
was above the reference's eighty

87
00:03:13.366 --> 00:03:14.790
on all sixty workloads.

88
00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.340
So it could not pass, whatever its time.

89
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.257
That ends the Bioinformatics lecture

90
00:03:21.257 --> 00:03:22.520
with three lessons.

91
00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.878
Speed comes from structure

92
00:03:24.878 --> 00:03:27.110
and every answer stays exact.

93
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:30.256
Memory and time trade against each other

94
00:03:30.256 --> 00:03:32.711
two rows cost about twice the time

95
00:03:32.711 --> 00:03:35.780
and storing more is faster but costs memory.

96
00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:37.006
And a small

97
00:03:37.006 --> 00:03:39.853
noisy measurement must win everywhere

98
00:03:39.853 --> 00:03:41.970
one lost workload is a fail.

99
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.577
The interactive notes for this lecture

100
00:03:44.636 --> 00:03:46.240
have every program we used.

101
00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.030
Next time: Lecture 7, machine learning.
